================================================================================================================================
ITEM 52 -- the tilt of the Fundamental Plane: does a_0 produce it?
================================================================================================================================
  6dFGS FP catalogue: 11102 rows, 7949 pass the quality cut (J-band flag Js=1, e_log sigma < 0.10, cz > 3000 km/s)
  [PASS] 52.0 UNITS CONTROL (can fail) -- rebuilding the tabulated mean effective surface brightness from the total J magnitude, the angular effective radius and the redshift reproduces it with small scatter, which pins BOTH conventions this item needs: R_e is tabulated in h^-1 kpc (H0 = 100) and I_e is the MEAN surface brightness inside R_e (L = 2 pi I_e R_e^2).  If either were wrong every number below would be wrong   (log10(I_e rebuilt / I_e tabulated) = -0.032 +- 0.027 dex over 7949 galaxies (the small mean offset is the k-correction and Galactic extinction Campbell+2014 apply and I did not))
  
dimensionless Jeans solution: sigma_ap = F(x) sqrt(G M_*/R_e).  Newtonian limit F = 0.4176 at aperture R_e/8 (equivalently a virial coefficient 1/F^2 = 5.73, against Cappellari+2006's beta(n=4) = 5.93)
  [PASS] 52.1 SCALE-FREENESS CONTROL (can fail) -- the framework's predicted dispersion really is sqrt(G M/R_e) times a function of x alone: two galaxies a decade apart in mass but at the same x give the same reduced dispersion.  That is what makes 'a = 2 exactly' a theorem rather than an approximation   (M=1e11/R_e=3.16 kpc gives sigma = 155.4 km/s, M=1e12/R_e=10 kpc gives 276.4 km/s; reduced dispersions agree to 9.6e-17 dex)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.  THE OBSERVED PLANE, AND WHAT EACH LAW PREDICTS FOR IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                  NOISELESS   a        b   |    WITH sigma ERRORS   a        b   |  orthogonal a        b
  framework, canonical a_0                2.002   -0.966   |                    1.095   -0.835   |         1.591   -0.932
  framework, alt a_0                      2.002   -0.957   |                    1.095   -0.830   |         1.591   -0.925
  framework, 'simple' mu                  2.002   -0.952   |                    1.095   -0.827   |         1.590   -0.921
  Newton + homology                       2.000   -1.000   |                    1.096   -0.854   |         1.588   -0.960
  Newton + Sersic non-homology            1.662   -0.946   |                    0.977   -0.835   |         1.524   -0.957
  OBSERVED                                   --       --   |                    0.769   -0.734   |         1.212   -0.810
  [PASS] 52.2 THE FRAMEWORK'S THEOREM, VERIFIED NUMERICALLY -- with a homologous family the kernel's argument depends on surface brightness alone, so the framework leaves the sigma coefficient at the exact virial 2 and puts the whole of its tilt into the surface-brightness coefficient.  The noiseless fits reproduce a = 2.000 for both the virial model and the framework, to three decimals   (virial (a, b) = (2.0000, -1.0000) -- the exact plane recovered to 1e-4; framework (2.0024, -0.9656): a moved by +0.0024, b by +0.0344)
  the framework's a is 2.0024 rather than exactly 2 for a reason worth stating, because it is not numerical: the framework's
  relation is log R_e = 2 log sigma - log I_e - 2 log F(I_e) + const, and log F is CURVED in log I_e.  A plane fitted to a curved
  surface leaks a little of that curvature into the other coefficient, because sigma and I_e are correlated across the sample.
  The leak is 0.002, three orders below the tilt being explained, and it vanishes exactly in the a_0/100 mutation where F is flat.
  [PASS] 52.3 PIPELINE VALIDATION AGAINST THE PUBLISHED VALUE (can fail) -- de-attenuating the direct fit of the real data by the attenuation the virial mock suffers from the galaxies' own sigma errors recovers the published maximum-likelihood 6dFGS J-band coefficient of Magoulas+2012 (a = 1.404) to better than 1%.  The fitting machinery is therefore doing what it should   (attenuation factor 0.548 from the virial mock (a: 2.000 noiseless -> 1.096 noisy); observed direct a = 0.769 -> de-attenuated 1.403 against Magoulas+2012's 1.404)
  [PASS] 52.4 AGAINST INTEREST, AND THIS IS THE ITEM'S ANSWER (the check ASSERTS THE FAILURE) -- the observed plane's SIGMA coefficient is far from virial, and the framework cannot move it at all: a_0 tilts the surface-brightness axis and leaves the sigma axis untouched, by the theorem in 52.2.  The item's bar was 'tilt within 0.05 of prediction'; the framework misses the sigma coefficient by six times that.  MOND at R_e is NOT the tilt of the Fundamental Plane   (observed a = 0.769, framework a = 1.095, virial a = 1.096: the framework misses by 0.326 (bar 0.05) and explains 0% of the a tilt.  On b it supplies 16% of the observed tilt (observed -0.734, framework -0.835, virial -0.854))
  the exponential kernel makes this item HARDER for the framework than plain MOND does: the same calculation with the
  'simple' mu gives b = -0.827, i.e. 22% of the b tilt against Route A's 16%, because nu - 1 = exp(-sqrt(y)) dies far faster above the transition than 1/(2y) does.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.  THE SAME STATEMENT AS AN M/L TILT, AND WHERE THE TILT ACTUALLY LIVES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                      d log(M/L)/d log L   p (at fixed I_e)   q (at fixed L)
  OBSERVED                                         0.079              0.020           -0.180
  framework, canonical a_0                         0.009             -0.005           -0.044
  framework, alt a_0                               0.012             -0.006           -0.053
  framework, 'simple' mu                           0.014             -0.005           -0.058
  Newton + homology                               -0.001             -0.004           -0.009
  Newton + Sersic non-homology                    -0.014             -0.019           -0.016
  p is the residual M/L trend with luminosity AT FIXED SURFACE BRIGHTNESS.  The framework's p is ZERO BY CONSTRUCTION --
  its kernel argument x = 2 pi G Upsilon I_e / a_0 contains no luminosity -- so p is the axis on which this item is decided.
  [PASS] 52.5 THE STRUCTURE IS RIGHT AND THE AMPLITUDE IS NOT (and this is the honest surprise of the item) -- the observed mass-to-light residual is dominated by SURFACE BRIGHTNESS, not by luminosity, exactly the structure the framework predicts: at fixed I_e the observed trend with L is small and close to the framework's zero.  But on the surface-brightness axis itself the framework delivers only a fraction of the observed coefficient   (p: observed +0.020 vs framework -0.005 (virial -0.004) -- a 0.026 miss; q: observed -0.180 vs framework -0.044 (virial -0.009) -- a 0.136 miss, i.e. the framework supplies 21% of the surface-brightness tilt)
  
BIAS CONTROL on q, against interest: R_e and I_e are fitted jointly at (nearly) fixed total magnitude, so an error in
  log R_e moves log I_e by about -2x and log(M_5/L) by +1x, which fakes a NEGATIVE q.  With the catalogue's own median
  e_log R_e = 0.048 dex against an intrinsic spread of 0.200 dex, the error fraction is 0.06 and the
  spurious contribution is about -0.028 -- so the true q is nearer -0.161 than -0.180, and the framework's share
  of it rises from 21% to about 23%.  Even so it is a minority of the tilt.

